**Debounce** delays a call until activity stops for `wait` ms (search box typing). **Throttle** allows at most one call per interval (scroll or resize handlers).

```js
function debounce(fn, wait) {
  let t;
  return function (...args) {
    clearTimeout(t);
    t = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), wait);
  };
}
function throttle(fn, limit) {
  let last = 0;
  return function (...args) {
    const now = Date.now();
    if (now - last >= limit) { last = now; fn.apply(this, args); }
  };
}
```
Both rely on a closure holding timer or timestamp state, and `apply` preserves `this` and arguments.

**Polyfill examples.**
```js
Function.prototype.myBind = function (ctx, ...pre) {
  const fn = this;
  return function (...args) { return fn.apply(ctx, [...pre, ...args]); };
};
Array.prototype.myMap = function (cb, thisArg) {
  const out = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < this.length; i++)
    if (i in this) out[i] = cb.call(thisArg, this[i], i, this);
  return out;
};
```
Mention the edge cases: sparse arrays, `new` on a bound function, and callback type checks.

**Promises.** A promise is pending, then fulfilled or rejected once. `then` returns a new promise, so chains flow and a thrown error skips to the next `catch`. `Promise.all` rejects on the first rejection; `allSettled` waits for all; `race` takes the first to settle. Callbacks run as microtasks, before timers.

**Currying** turns `f(a, b, c)` into `f(a)(b)(c)`:
```js
const curry = fn => function c(...a) {
  return a.length >= fn.length ? fn(...a) : (...b) => c(...a, ...b);
};
```

**Regex.** Know character classes, quantifiers, anchors, groups and flags. Example: `/^\d{6}$/` tests a 6-digit OTP; `str.replace(/\s+/g, " ")` collapses whitespace.

**Tip.** Talk through the closure and edge cases while coding; interviewers grade explanation as much as output.
